In article <555@uucs1.UUCP> rfp@uucs1.UUCP (Roger F. Powell) writes:
>
>At the risk of using the "M" word (Moria) in this group, let me go on
>record as saying that Moria does a much better job on this issue than
>our beloved nethack.  To summarize:  [ snip ]
>
>Spells may be cast as many times as mana will allow - the spell never
>gets "overused".  What keeps this whole thing in balance is that the
>really good spells take a fair amount of mana, and mana doesn't regenerate
>too quickly.  Further balance is added by the fact that only wizards/priests
>can cast the highest spells/prayers (and only when they are very high level).

Actually, it's pretty unbalanced.  The reason is that Mages get some _very_
useful spells very early on (identify, for example).  All one has to do is
kill off all the nearby monsters, cast identify until it works or you run out
of mana, and then rest for a while to regenerate your mana.  There's no problem
of starving, because you have your handy "create food" spell when you get
hungry.  There are other tricks too, like alternately recharging two identical
wands until one of them blows up; I once got the other one up to +143 before
this happened.  If spells can be used forever, the spellbook of identify (at
least) would have to go.

>Now Moria has an implicit design that is oriented toward archieving the
>ends differently, based on the character type.

Then how come everyone who wins always wins using a mage?

>                                                It is very hard to "max
>out" your stats - much harder than in nethack.

Once again, everyone who wins has their stats maxed out.  (Except for the ones
that can't be changed, and they aren't as useful anyway.)  Whether you can
max out your stats is mainly a function of how long you can endure the mindless
tedium of searching for gain stat potions.

I do think that the spell system in Nethack sucks, though.  I've never found
much use for spells.  But lets not overdo it, and make spells so useful that
they make the game too easy.
